    {
      "id": "problem",
      "title": "Why sequential models are too slow",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "problem-1",
          "say": "This document proposes a new translation architecture and asks how to escape step-by-step recurrent computation. Its main claims are attention-only sequence transduction, significantly faster parallelizable training, and new state-of-the-art translation quality.",
          "actions": [
            {
              "type": "focus",
              "target": "p2.s5"
            },
            {
              "type": "highlight",
              "targets": [
                "p2.s5"
              ],
              "color": "yellow",
              "at_word": 11
            },
            {
              "type": "highlight",
              "targets": [
                "p2.s6"
              ],
              "color": "yellow",
              "at_word": 12
            },
            {
              "type": "highlight",
              "targets": [
                "p2.s8"
              ],
              "color": "yellow",
              "at_word": 11
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim-1",
      "title": "Attention-only sequence transduction model",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "idea-1",
          "say": "The conclusion is that a full encoder-decoder translator can be built from attention alone. Because recurrent and convolutional layers are replaced by stacked multi-head self-attention, global dependencies are drawn directly between input and output positions without step-by-step recurrence.",
          "actions": [
            {
              "type": "focus",
              "target": "p2.s24"
            },
            {
              "type": "highlight",
              "targets": [
                "p2.s24"
              ],
              "color": "yellow",
              "at_word": 10
            },
            {
              "type": "highlight",
              "targets": [
                "p2.s25"
              ],
              "color": "yellow",
              "at_word": 22
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "method-1",
          "say": "Stacked attention works because each layer splits its work. The board shows one head's slice as an illustrative special case. A wide input row meets a tall projection, so width 512 becomes width 64 for that head. Eight such heads run side by side in different learned subspaces, then rejoin to width 512 at similar cost to one large head. Feed-forward layers then process each position separately.",
          "actions": [
            {
              "type": "focus",
              "target": "p3.s2"
            },
            {
              "type": "board",
              "item": {
                "kind": "shapes",
                "title": "One head projects 512 to 64",
                "terms": [
                  {
                    "kind": "matrix",
                    "name": "X",
                    "rows": "n",
                    "cols": "512"
                  },
                  {
                    "kind": "op",
                    "op": "times"
                  },
                  {
                    "kind": "matrix",
                    "name": "W^Q_i",
                    "rows": "512",
                    "cols": "64"
                  },
                  {
                    "kind": "op",
                    "op": "equals"
                  },
                  {
                    "kind": "matrix",
                    "name": "head_i",
                    "rows": "n",
                    "cols": "64"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "highlight",
              "targets": [
                "p3.s2"
              ],
              "color": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "board_focus",
              "part": 0,
              "at_word": 21
            },
            {
              "type": "board_focus",
              "part": 2,
              "at_word": 32
            }
          ],
          "hold_ms": 800
        },
        {
          "id": "figure-1",
          "say": "The Transformer handles translation with attention alone and no recurrence or convolution. Because its encoder and decoder stacks use only multi-head attention and feed-forward blocks. The test is WMT 2014 translation measured in BLEU: 28.4 on English-to-German, over 2 BLEU above the previous best including ensembles, and 41.8 on English-to-French as a new single-model best. That covers those two text tasks, not other modalities.",
          "actions": [
            {
